<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bartolomé de las Casas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1484–<a href="/years/1566">1566</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bartolomé de las Casas was a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> of the sixteenth century who became a leading critic of the treatment of indigenous peoples in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. His writings denounced abuses by colonists and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> system. He influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws of 1542</a>, which sought to reform the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s colonial governance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas.jpg" alt="Bartolomé de las Casas" width="310" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous / Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fray_Bartolom%C3%A9_de_las_Casas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the University of Salamanca, Francisco de Vitoria and his successors developed ideas of natural law, the rights of peoples and economics that influenced international law, and the debate at Valladolid in 1550–1551 between <b>Bartolomé de las Casas</b> and Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda examined the justice of conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dominican friar <b>Bartolomé de las Casas</b>'s campaign against the abuse of indigenous peoples helped produce the New Laws of 1542, which sought, with mixed success, to curb the encomienda and outlaw the enslavement of indigenous Americans, and in the Valladolid debate of 1550–1551 he argued against Juan…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>What is known of the Taíno comes from Spanish chroniclers such as <b>Bartolomé de las Casas</b> and the friar Ramón Pané, from excavated villages, ball courts and carvings, and from DNA studies of ancient and living people.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dominican friar <b>Bartolomé de las Casas</b> denounced the destruction of the Caribbean peoples in his Short Account of the Destruction of the Indies (written 1542, printed 1552), the theologian Francisco de Vitoria questioned the grounds of Spanish dominion in his lectures of 1539, and at Valladolid in…</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Aristotle himself was cited in its defence: at the Valladolid debate of 1550–1551 Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda justified the Spanish conquest with Aristotle's theory of natural slavery, against <b>Bartolomé de las Casas</b>, who had denounced the destruction of the Caribbean peoples.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bartolomé de las Casas</b> led peaceful missions in Verapaz.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-guatemala">Captaincy General of Guatemala</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.39,-6&amp;year=1537" aria-label="Seville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4400 818 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bartolomé de las Casas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4850 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4850 1088h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.39,-6&amp;year=1537">What was here in 1537? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now this infinite multitude of Men are by the Creation of God innocently simple, altogether void of and averse to all manner of Craft, Subtlety and Malice…&#8221;</p><footer>A Brief Account of the Destruction of the Indies (1552) &#183; anonymous translation, London 1689 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20321/pg20321-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the Spaniards never received any injury from the Indians, but that they rather reverenced them as Persons descended from Heaven…&#8221;</p><footer>A Brief Account of the Destruction of the Indies (1552) &#183; anonymous translation, London 1689 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20321/pg20321-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Spaniards first assaulted the innocent Sheep, so qualified by the Almighty, as is premention'd, like most cruel Tygers, Wolves and Lions hunger-starv'd…&#8221;</p><footer>A Brief Account of the Destruction of the Indies (1552) &#183; anonymous translation, London 1689 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20321/pg20321-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I my self have heard the Spaniards themselves (who dare not assume the Confidence to deny the good Nature praedominant in them) declare, that there was nothing wanting in them for the acquisition of Eternal Beatitude, but the sole Knowledge and Understanding of the Deity.&#8221;</p><footer>A Brief Account of the Destruction of the Indies (1552) &#183; anonymous translation, London 1689 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20321/pg20321-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Finally, in one word, their Ambition and Avarice, than which the heart of Man never entertained greater…&#8221;</p><footer>A Brief Account of the Destruction of the Indies (1552) &#183; anonymous translation, London 1689 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20321/pg20321-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is no Tongue, Art, or Human knowledge can recite the horrid Impieties, which these Capital Enemies to Government and all Mankind have been guilty of at several times and in several Nations…&#8221;</p><footer>A Brief Account of the Destruction of the Indies (1552), on New Spain &#183; anonymous translation, London 1689 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/20321/pg20321-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
